  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather conditions are expected through\nthe end of the week.\n\nHigh pressure will shift offshore tonight allowing for a\nslightly warmer night as winds become slightly elevated. \nHowever, many locations will still be in the lower to mid 40s, \nwith some upper 30s possible in the more sheltered valleys given\nthe dry nature of the air mass. Given the cool temperatures, \nsome patchy river valley fog is possible, especially west of the\nBlue Ridge. Winds turn southerly Wednesday allowing temps to \nclimb back into the 70s. Temperatures warm up to near 80 \nThursday before the passage of another dry cold front. Temps \ncool off slightly for the end of the week as weak high pressure \nof Canadian origin builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increased rain potential late this weekend into\nearly next week. \n\nThe National Hurricane Center is currently monitoring a disturbance \nover the southwestern Gulf of America (TD 9). It is anticipated\nthat moisture associated with what is now TD 9 will move into \nour region later this weekend or early next week. There are a \nlot of possibilities for where this system goes, and by \nextension, the potential impacts that could bring to our area. \nThere will be a few key features ahead of this system to watch, \nbut the most important will be a cutoff low expected to develop \nover TX. Cutoff lows are notoriously difficult to forecast, \nhence, the level of uncertainty with the forecast. At this \njuncture, several scenarios appear possible:\n\n(1) Quick-moving low pressure moves onshore of the Gulf Coast, and \nthreads the needle between high pressure over the Southwest Atlantic \nand the Upper Midwest, allowing it to move further north toward the \nOH Valley, central Appalachians and Mid-Atlantic, delivering notably \nhigher rain potential and amounts locally.\n\n(2) A very quick-moving low pressure system is forced to make an \neastward turn sooner across the Southeast prior to the western \nextent of SW Atlantic ridging exiting the coast. This would keep a \nmajority of moisture and rain chances to our south, though some \nmoisture could still be drawn northward along a passing front.\n\n(3) A slow-moving low pressure system is forced to make that \neastward turn sooner, blocked in this scenario by the second area of \nhigh pressure in the Upper Midwest building in before it can trek \ntoo far to the north. This would keep nearly all precipitation and \nmoisture associated with the low suppressed to the south.\n\n(4) A very slow-moving area of low pressure would not only get \ncaught beneath ridging building over the Upper Midwest, but could \nthen remain cutoff from the large scale steering pattern. This would \nleave low pressure meandering somewhere well to our south for a \ntime, before an uncertain exit beyond the current forecast period.\n\nAt the moment, deterministic and ensemble medium range guidance \nexhibits very large spread, falling into (but not necessarily \nfavoring) any one of the four scenarios listed above.\n\nIt will be important to stay tuned to the forecast over the next few \ndays, and monitor hurricanes.gov for details on any potential \nfurther development of TD 9.",
